                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881690,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 26.005001,
                  "from": 21.925,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881700,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 81.04,
                  "from": 76.240005,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2881710,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Hi. Today we're going to be looking at animals in urban environments, and I'm going to be telling you about some research on how they're affected by these environments. Now in evolutionary terms, urban environments represent huge upheavals, the sorts of massive changes that usually happen over millions of years. And we used to think that only a few species could adapt to this new environment. One species, is which well known as being highly adaptable is the crow, and there have been various studies about how they manage to learn new skills.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2881711,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Hi.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 84.64,
                  "from": 83.68,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881713,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Today we're going to be looking at animals in urban environments, and I'm going to be telling you about some research on how they're affected by these environments.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 95.945,
                  "from": 85.12,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881742,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Now in evolutionary terms, urban environments represent huge upheavals, the sorts of massive changes that usually happen over millions of years.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 108.25,
                  "from": 97.225,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881764,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And we used to think that only a few species could adapt to this new environment.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 114.329994,
                  "from": 109.13,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881781,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One species, is which well known as being highly adaptable is the crow, and there have been various studies about how they manage to learn new skills.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 125.98499,
                  "from": 115.13,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2881809,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Another successful species is the pigeon because they're able to perch on ledges on the walls of city buildings, just like they once perched on cliffs by the sea. But in fact, we're now finding that these early immigrants were just the start of a more general movement of animals into cities and of adaptation by these animals to city life. And one thing that researchers are finding especially interesting is the speed with which they're doing this. We're not talking about gradual evolution here. These animals are changing fast.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2881810,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Another successful species is the pigeon because they're able to perch on ledges on the walls of city buildings, just like they once perched on cliffs by the sea.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 137.85,
                  "from": 126.784996,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881840,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "But in fact, we're now finding that these early immigrants were just the start of a more general movement of animals into cities and of adaptation by these animals to city life.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 151.16501,
                  "from": 138.65,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881873,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And one thing that researchers are finding especially interesting is the speed with which they're doing this.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 158.90001,
                  "from": 151.885,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881891,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "We're not talking about gradual evolution here.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 162.42001,
                  "from": 159.14,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881899,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "These animals are changing fast.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 165.46,
                  "from": 162.58,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2881905,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Let me tell you about some of the studies that have been carried out in this area. So in the University of Minnesota, a biologist called Emily Snellrude and her colleagues looked at specimens of urbanized small mammals such as mice and gophers that had been collected in Minnesota, and that are now kept in museums there. And she looked at specimens that had been collected over the last hundred years, which is a very short time in evolutionary terms. And she found that during that time, these small mammals had experienced a jump in brain size when compared to rural mammals. Now we can't be sure this means they're more intelligent, but since the sizes of other parts of the body didn't change, it does suggest that something cognitive was going on.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2881906,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Let me tell you about some of the studies that have been carried out in this area.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 170.90001,
                  "from": 166.74,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881924,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "So in the University of Minnesota, a biologist called Emily Snellrude and her colleagues looked at specimens of urbanized small mammals such as mice and gophers that had been collected in Minnesota, and that are now kept in museums there.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 190.68001,
                  "from": 172.02501,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881965,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And she looked at specimens that had been collected over the last hundred years, which is a very short time in evolutionary terms.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 199.08,
                  "from": 191.16,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2881989,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And she found that during that time, these small mammals had experienced a jump in brain size when compared to rural mammals.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 208.30501,
                  "from": 199.74501,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2882012,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Now we can't be sure this means they're more intelligent, but since the sizes of other parts of the body didn't change, it does suggest that something cognitive was going on.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 220.82,
                  "from": 208.785,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2882044,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "And Snellrude thinks that this change might reflect the cognitive demands of adjusting to city life, having to look in different places to find food, for example, and coping with a whole new set of dangers. Then over in Germany, at the Max Planck Institute, there's another biologist called Katharina Miranda, who's done some experiments with blackbirds living in urban and rural areas, and she's been looking not at their anatomy but at their behavior. So as you might expect, she's found that the urban blackbirds tend to be quite bold. They're prepared to face up to a lot of threats that would frighten away their country counterparts. But there's one type of situation that does seem to frighten the urban blackbirds, and that's anything new, anything they haven't experienced before.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2882045,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And Snellrude thinks that this change might reflect the cognitive demands of adjusting to city life, having to look in different places to find food, for example, and coping with a whole new set of dangers.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 235.645,
                  "from": 221.62,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2882082,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Then over in Germany, at the Max Planck Institute, there's another biologist called Katharina Miranda, who's done some experiments with blackbirds living in urban and rural areas, and she's been looking not at their anatomy but at their behavior.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 254.98,
                  "from": 237.005,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2882122,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "So as you might expect, she's found that the urban blackbirds tend to be quite bold.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 261.375,
                  "from": 255.62,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2882139,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "They're prepared to face up to a lot of threats that would frighten away their country counterparts.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 267.05502,
                  "from": 261.855,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2882157,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "But there's one type of situation that does seem to frighten the urban blackbirds, and that's anything new, anything they haven't experienced before.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 277.77002,
                  "from": 267.45502,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2882181,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "And if you think about it, that's quite sensible for a bird living in the city. Jonathan Atwell in Indiana University is looking at how a range of animals respond to urban environments. He's found that when they're under stress, their endocrine systems react by reducing the amount of hormones such as corticosterone into their blood. It's a sensible seeming adaptation. A rat that gets scared every time a subway train rolls past won't be very successful.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2882182,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And if you think about it, that's quite sensible for a bird living in the city.",
                "meta": {
